### Memory Corruption Demonstration
```javascript
const { heap_size } = require('heap');

// Simulating a vulnerable state
var vulnerable = new object[heap_size];

// Input: A large string meant for a small variable
var input = 'INPUT_VAR = '" + vulnerable[0] + '"' + vulnerable[1] + '""';

// Manipulating the heap
const buffer = heap.new(heap_size);
input.push(`${input}`);
// Overwriting the first few bytes
buffer[0] = '0xBADF';  // Overwrite with null
buffer[1] = '0x00';  // Overwrite with null

// Executing the payload
console.log(buffer[1]);

// Restoring heap
heap.clear(heap_size);
```
This shows that when an application lets users (or attackers) control the heap memory, the original logic breaks.

